microtun for Debian
===================

This package installs one Linux executable:

  /usr/bin/microtun

Host functionality is selected with subcommands:

  microtun tunnel [-i mt0] /path/to/device.toml
  microtun tracker /etc/microtun/tracker.toml
  microtun console serial-gateway.example --port 2217
  microtun serial serial-gateway.example --port 2217 --name ttyMT0

The Tracker service is installed as microtun-tracker.service, but package
installation deliberately does not enable or start it. The packaged
/etc/microtun/tracker.toml is an example and must be reviewed before first use.
It contains a private tunnel key placeholder and should remain readable only by
root and the dedicated microtun-tracker service group.

After configuring the Tracker:

  systemctl enable --now microtun-tracker.service
  systemctl status microtun-tracker.service

The package creates a dedicated microtun-tracker system user and group for the
service. The service does not run as root.

The `microtun tunnel` command is intentionally not installed as a system-wide
service: a Linux host may have multiple device configurations and interface
names. A sample device configuration is installed at:

  /usr/share/doc/microtun/examples/device.toml

For example:

  microtun tunnel -i mt0 /path/to/device.toml

Serial console
--------------

`microtun console` switches into an interactive serial-console mode from inside
the TUI. Connect to the serial endpoint and press Ctrl-A P to open communication
parameters. Enable serial mode on the first row:

  microtun console serial-gateway.example --port 2217

Entering serial mode does not change the remote UART configuration. The serial
protocol queries the current settings and applies changes only when requested, so
an already-correct serial console can start displaying logging data immediately.
Use Ctrl-A F for BREAK; turn the first row in Ctrl-A P back off to return to
normal Telnet mode. Matching Minicom shortcuts are also used for Ctrl-A S (send),
Ctrl-A C (clear), Ctrl-A Q (quit), and Ctrl-A Z (help). Ctrl-A R retains Minicom's
receive-file meaning, but receiving files is not implemented yet.

Virtual serial device
---------------------

For programs that require a normal Linux serial character device, `microtun serial`
bridges a serial connection through CUSE. Load the kernel module before use:

  modprobe cuse
  microtun serial serial-gateway.example --port 2217 --name ttyMT0

The resulting /dev/ttyMT0 accepts normal termios and serial ioctls; baud,
data bits, parity, stop bits, flow control, DTR/RTS, break, and flush requests
are translated into serial commands. Device-node ownership and mode are governed
by the host's normal device/udev policy.
